What was rewritten: the plugin's one job - keep a row for every reaction
change in the chats the bot serves, and answer /reaction-log with which emoji
are used, on how many messages, in how many chats, and when last - as a
Nodecode cell. Its hashing carries over unchanged: a chat is 12 hex digits of
a SHA-256 over room and chat id, a message 12 hex digits over room, chat and
message id, so no chat or message id, no user id and no user name is ever
written. Its JSON-lines file under the data directory, its newest-rows trim,
its options (/reaction-log all | Nh | Nd | days | recent [N] | clear, and its
one-line refusal of an option it does not know) and its rule that each
message counts once with the reactions it has now all carry over. Where the
plugin subscribed to Hermes's gateway_platform_event hook, the cell advises
the channel kit's reaction seam, which every room adapter hands its reactions
to; the kit loads as a cell of its own, so the advice is taken when the seam
exists and a worker keeps looking while it does not. It still only watches: an
error in the observer is swallowed, and the kit's own answer stands. The same
three verbs are on the manual as reaction-ledger:summary, reaction-ledger:recent
and reaction-ledger:clear for a report taken through eval.

What was left out, and why: the plugin kept the reactions a message carries
after each change, because Telegram hands over the whole set each time; a
kit candidate is one emoji added or taken back, so a row here is that one
change and a message's current reactions are its rows folded in file order.
The Unicode-name column of the plugin's table is gone, since this image has
no Unicode name table, and with it the custom-emoji count: the kit reads
plain emoji only. The plugin's 2 MB byte bound is the section's row count
instead, since one row's size no longer varies with a chat's name. The
plugin's own card image and its end-to-end harness are not part of the cell.
